<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.berwilson.cron-task-sheet</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/zsh</string>
    <string>-c</string>
    <string>curl -sS -m 280 -H "Authorization: Bearer $(grep '^CRON_SECRET=' __APP_DIR__/.env.local | cut -d= -f2-)" http://localhost:3000/api/cron/task-sheet</string>
  </array>
  <!-- Every 15 minutes, all day. Replaced com.berwilson.cron-google-tasks on
       2026-10-11, at the same cadence and for the same reason: three confirm
       routes (meeting intake, email ingestion, action items) create tasks in
       bulk without going through /api/tasks, so they never fire the immediate
       refresh, and "the tasks someone assigned you this morning turn up
       tomorrow" is the wrong answer for a to-do list.

       It now bounds a second thing the Google Tasks sync did not have to care
       about: how long a TICKED CHECKBOX waits before it settles. Google
       notified us of nothing either way, but a person who ticks a box and sees
       the platform disagree ten minutes later has learned the sync is a lie.

       Note what this interval does NOT govern: anything edited inside the
       platform reaches the sheet in seconds via queueTaskSheetRefresh.

       A run is three Sheets calls and one Drive permissions read against a
       46-row board — it touches no local model and so competes with nothing.
       LM Studio serves one request at a time (§12), which is why the jobs that
       DO use it are spread across the morning and this one need not be.

       If you change it, change SYNC_INTERVAL_MINUTES in src/lib/system-health.ts
       to match, or the health card reports a healthy sync as stalled.

       StartInterval rather than the StartCalendarInterval most jobs here use,
       because those are daily or weekly and this one is not.

       ThrottleInterval matches, so a run that overruns cannot stack — and the
       curl -m 280 ceiling is under both it and the route's own maxDuration of
       300, so a wedged run cannot straddle the next tick. -->
  <key>StartInterval</key>
  <integer>900</integer>
  <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
  <integer>900</integer>
  <key>AbandonProcessGroup</key>
  <false/>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>__LOG_DIR__/cron-task-sheet.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>__LOG_DIR__/cron-task-sheet.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
